 *** Revised URR parameters 19 May 2010 ************************* 2525 1451  415
 **************************************************************** 2525 1451  416
                                                                  2525 1451  417
ORNL 2007-2008                                                    2525 1451  418
                                                                  2525 1451  419
EVALUATION of the RESONANCE PARAMETERS of Mn-55 in the ENERGY     2525 1451  420
RANGE 0 to 122 keV- H. Derrien, L.C. Leal, N.M.Larson, D. Wiarda  2525 1451  421
K. Guber, and G. Arbanas.                                         2525 1451  422
                                                                  2525 1451  423
The resonance parameters were obtained from a sequential Bayes    2525 1451  424
SAMMY analysis of the most recent experimental neutron            2525 1451  425
transmissions and capture cross sections in the resolved energy   2525 1451  426
range: Harvey et al. transmissions measured at ORELA in 1980,     2525 1451  427
Aerts et al. Capture cross sections measured at GELINA in 2006,   2525 1451  428
and Guber et al. Capture cross section measured at ORELA in 2007. 2525 1451  429
For the evaluation in the thermal energy range Widder et al.      2525 1451  430
(1975) capture cross section, Cote et al. (1964) total cross      2525 1451  431
sections, and Rainwater et al. (1964) total cross sections were   2525 1451  432
considered. The thermal capture cross sections were normalized at 2525 1451  433
the 0.0253 eV value of 13.27 b which is an average of the         2525 1451  434
available experimental values, consistent with the recent         2525 1451  435
evaluation of Trkov et al.(2007).  The thermal cross sections     2525 1451  436
calculated by the resonance parameters are the following:         2525 1451  437
                                                Res. Int.         2525 1451  438
                   Capture        13.274 b       13.514           2525 1451  439
                   Total          15.390 b         -              2525 1451  440
                   Scattering      2.116 b         -              2525 1451  441
                                                                  2525 1451  442
The capture resonance integral is 13.514 b in the energy range    2525 1451  443
0.5 eV to 120 keV.                                                2525 1451  444
                                                                  2525 1451  445
Most of the p-wave resonances are not observed in the experimental2525 1451  446
transmission data. For those resonances only the capture area     2525 1451  447
could be obtained with good accuracy.  The capture area is        2525 1451  448
proportional to   gamman*gammag / gammatot and only the product   2525 1451  449
gamman*gammag could be defined.  A solution is to assign to all   2525 1451  450
this resonances a constant capture width value equal to an        2525 1451  451
estimated averaged value.  In this case the SAMMY fit to the      2525 1451  452
capture data is generally poor.  The solution of varying both the 2525 1451  453
capture width and the neutron width in the SAMMY fit has been     2525 1451  454
chosen is the evaluation.  The resulting capture widths do not    2525 1451  455
deviate too much from an average value.  The fit is obviously not 2525 1451  456
unique but give a good description of the experimental data. The  2525 1451  457
study of the statistical properties of the neutron widths has     2525 1451  458
shown that the obtained set of parameters is reasonable.          2525 1451  459
                                                                  2525 1451  460
Only s-wave and p-wave resonances are given in the evaluation.    2525 1451  461
However the study of the statistical properties of the p-wave     2525 1451  462
resonance parameters has shown that 25% of them(the very small    2525 1451  463
one) could be d-wave resonances. Including these possible d-wave  2525 1451  464
resonances in the p-wave resonances has a negligible effect on    2525 1451  465
the calculated capture cross section.                             2525 1451  466
                                                                  2525 1451  467
Two components of the capture cross section are given is the      2525 1451  468
evaluation: the first component is calculated by the observed     2525 1451  469
resonance parameters; the second component is given as a File 3   2525 1451  470
background.  This background stands for                           2525 1451  471
the contribution of the direct capture, for the contribution of   2525 1451  472
non observed d-wave resonances, and for possible errors           2525 1451  473
in the calculation of the multiple scattering effect by           2525 1451  474
SAMMY in the experimental capture cross sections. The estimation  2525 1451  475
of the average d-wave contribution below 122 keV was obtained from2525 1451  476
SAMMY/URR.  The error on the experimental background is           2525 1451  477
important(20% to 30%), contributing to the most part of           2525 1451  478
the uncertainty on the average capture cross section              2525 1451  479
                                                                  2525 1451  480
The following table compares the average capture cross section to 2525 1451  481
the experimental values of Lerigoleur , and of Garg and Macklin.  2525 1451  482
The present evaluation is systematically lower than Lerigoleur    2525 1451  483
(absolute measurement) by 12% on average; part of the discrepancy 2525 1451  484
could be due to the inaccuracy of the calculation of the d-wave   2525 1451  485
component in the background file.                                 2525 1451  486
                                                                  2525 1451  487
                                                                  2525 1451  488
